{:now [{:claim "To every equation belongs a group of permutations of its roots"
        :modern "The Galois group Gal(L/K) of the splitting field L of a polynomial over K is the group of field automorphisms of L fixing K; it acts faithfully on the roots, so it is a subgroup of S_n."
        :tex "\\mathrm{Gal}(L/K) \\hookrightarrow S_n"}
       {:claim "Lowering the group by adjoining quantities"
        :modern "The fundamental theorem of Galois theory: intermediate fields K ⊂ M ⊂ L correspond one to one, inclusion reversed, to subgroups H of Gal(L/K); M/K is normal exactly when H is a normal subgroup."
        :tex "M \\mapsto \\mathrm{Gal}(L/M),\\qquad H \\mapsto L^H"}
       {:claim "Solvable by radicals exactly when the group decomposes properly down to prime orders"
        :modern "A polynomial in characteristic 0 is solvable by radicals if and only if its Galois group is solvable; S_n is solvable for n ≤ 4 and not for n ≥ 5, since A_5 is simple."
        :tex "G \\trianglerighteq G_1 \\trianglerighteq \\dots \\trianglerighteq 1,\\quad G_i/G_{i+1} \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/p_i"}]
 :fields [{:field :abstract-algebra :how "The group, the normal subgroup and the quotient became objects of their own through Jordan (1870), Cayley and Dedekind; the classification of finite simple groups (completed 2004) lists every possible indecomposable step." :example "A_5, the smallest non-abelian simple group, of order 60: Galois' 5.4.3."}
          {:field :number-theory :how "Galois groups of number fields organise algebraic number theory: class field theory describes the abelian ones, the Langlands programme aims at the rest." :example "Gal(Q(ζ_n)/Q) ≅ (Z/n)^*, Gauss' cyclotomy of 1801 seen through Galois."}
          {:field :geometry :how "Ruler and compass constructions are towers of square roots, so a length is constructible only when its Galois group has order a power of 2." :example "Wantzel 1837: doubling the cube and trisecting the angle are impossible."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Computing" :how "Computer algebra systems compute Galois groups of polynomials and decide solvability by radicals." :example "GAP, Magma and PARI/GP identify transitive groups of degree up to 47."}]
 :tangents [{:series :group-theory :hook "What a group is, its subgroups, normal subgroups and quotients: the language Galois invented to answer one question about equations." :link "planned"}
            {:series :erlangen :hook "Klein (1872) carried the same idea from equations to geometry: a geometry is what a group leaves unchanged." :link "/erlangen-program/"}]
